    "passage_base": "OpenSSL has not included NIST PQC algorithms in its official\nrelease, but has signaled that support is forthcoming now that\nstandards are finalized [19]. In September 2024, the OpenSSL\nFoundation announced plans to implement the NIST PQC finalists (Kyber, Dilithium, Falcon, SPHINCS+) in a future release,\nstating: “We intend to implement support for these algorithms\nin our providers in a future version of OpenSSL” [19]. This\ncame after NIST published the FIPS draft standards (ML-KEM,\nML-DSA, SLH-DSA), which OpenSSL was awaiting to avoid\nintegrating pre-standard algorithms. As of early 2025, integration is underway (targeting OpenSSL 3.5), but not yet released.\nMeanwhile, since early 2022, the Open Quantum Safe (OQS)\nproject has offered PQC support for OpenSSL 3 via its provider\narchitecture [34]. Maintained by openquantumsafe.org, the OQS\nprovider allowed experimentation with many PQC algorithms\nacross TLS 1.3 and X.509 [19], serving as a “test vehicle” for",
    "passage_both": "OpenSSL has not included NIST PQC algorithms in its official release, but has signaled that support is forthcoming now that standards are finalized. In September 2024, the OpenSSL Foundation announced plans to implement the NIST PQC finalists (Kyber, Dilithium, Falcon, SPHINCS+) in a future release, stating: “We intend to implement support for these algorithms in our providers in a future version of OpenSSL”. Maintained by openquantumsafe.org, the OQS provider allowed experimentation with many PQC algorithms across TLS 1.3 and X.509, serving as a “test vehicle” for",
